{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>They shall not make bald a baldness.</b> Over a dead person. But were not Israel too warned about this? Rather, since it is said of Israel בֵּין עֵינֵיכֶם (Deuteronomy 14), one might think he is not liable for the entire head — therefore Scripture says בְּרֹאשָׁם. And Israel learn from priests by a gezerah shavah: קָרְחָה is said here and קָרְחָה is said of Israel — just as here it means the entire head, so too there the entire head is implied — anywhere on the head that he makes bald; and just as there it is over a dead person, so too here over a dead person (Makkot 20)." "<b>And they shall not shave the edge of their beard.</b> Since it is said of Israel לֹא תַשְׁחִית, one might think he would be liable for plucking with a מַלְקֵט and a רְהִיטְנִי — therefore Scripture says לֹא יְגַלֵּחוּ. He is liable only for something called גִּלּוּחַ that also involves הַשְׁחָתָה — and that is a razor (ibid.)." "<b>And they shall not make a cut in their flesh.</b> Since it is said of Israel וְשֶׂרֶט לָנֶפֶשׁ לֹא תִתְּנוּ (Leviticus 19), one might think that if he made five cuts he would be liable for only one — therefore Scripture says לֹא יִשְׂרְטוּ שָׂרָטֶת, to make him liable for each and every cut. This word is extra for derash, since he could have written לֹא יִשְׂרְטוּ, and I already know it is a cut (Sifra; Makkot 20)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {1 "מַלְקֵט and רְהִיטְנִי appear to be terms for implements or methods of hair removal; rendered as in the original without expanding."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:07:35.650445838Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}